# Pinned verovio for the vendored, reproducible build.
#
# Single source of truth for `third_party/build-verovio.sh` and for the CI
# composite `.github/actions/verovio`, which reads VEROVIO_SHA into its cache
# key. Shell-sourceable: KEY=VALUE, no spaces, no quoting needed.

# Where the pinned commit is fetched from. Upstream: unlike Faust, our pin
# carries no patch of our own -- everything we need is already in the tree.
VEROVIO_ORIGIN=https://github.com/rism-digital/verovio

# The commit verovio is built from: the **6.4.1 release** (tag `version-6.4.1`,
# 2026-11-03). A release tag, moved forward as upstream releases: this project
# follows verovio's development and uses the library as it is, so a new release
# is taken rather than worked around (docs/decisions.md, "Verovio is followed,
# not adapted").
#
# Why one pin serves both ends: the page engraves with an Emscripten build of
# these same sources (`build-verovio-wasm.sh`, exporting the same C wrapper
# this repository links). Native and browser therefore engrave with one
# verovio, which is what lets the two clients' display lists be compared to
# each other rather than to two different engines. A repin moves both ends or
# neither.
#
# Why the previous pin was a commit, kept because it is the history of this
# file's shape: in 6.2.1 the score editor is dead in any build that keeps
# Humdrum support -- the PyPI wheel included: Toolkit::SetViewAndEditor() guards
# the construction of m_editorToolkit with `#if defined NO_HUMDRUM_SUPPORT`
# where it meant `#ifndef NO_EDIT_SUPPORT`, so the guard never opens, the
# pointer stays null, and every Toolkit::Edit() returns false (parameterless
# `undo` included, which is what makes the diagnosis certain -- the symbols are
# in the shipped .so, so it is not a NO_EDIT_SUPPORT build). Upstream inverted
# the guard in 8100cb396 ("Invert #define fix", 2026-05-27), so a build with a
# working editor meant pinning past it, ahead of any release. 6.3.0 carries the
# fix, which retires that whole argument: the published wheel is usable again
# and this build is no longer mandatory *for the editor's sake*. It stays the
# only build we use, for the reasons in BUILD-VEROVIO.md ("Why there is no
# Python-module target") -- a second compile, a second copy of the SMuFL data,
# and a distribution literally named `verovio` that pip can replace under us.
#
# Full 40-char SHA: fetching an arbitrary commit by SHA only resolves
# unabbreviated.
VEROVIO_SHA=fbe8f4695ab0a123cb096e0cf92354b44f7a2fb8
